ns \\( f(-5) = 4 \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = -5 \\) into the polynomial \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = k(-5)^4 + 9(-5)^3 + k(-5)^2 + 32(-5) - 11\n\\]\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,26313,979,400,74,87,61,19,10,24,87,61,18,45455,87,61,17,10,18,17,87,12,16,16,3,374,17779,553,400,87,488,220,20,3,374,400,19,12947,220,7379,400,74,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,686,990,279,91592,1107,576,13173,13,576,91592,1107,576,13173,5302,429,279,26313,315,279,12804,315,264,47311,17767,282,2075,8,1124,8,553,17767,856,481,272,1124,8,374,17767,282,1337,8,1124,568,758,419,1142,11,582,525,49702,553,17767,856,488,220,20,1124,701,892,646,387,5326,438,17767,856,481,10293,20,8,1124,701,773,17767,272,284,481,20,1124,3593,22043,279,47311,17767,282,2075,8,284,595,87,61,19,488,220,24,87,61,18,488,595,87,61,17,488,220,18,17,87,481,220,16,16,1124,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,897,315,17767,595,1124,8,1741,429,279,26313,979,17767,282,2075,8,1124,8,374,17779,553,17767,856,488,220,20,1124,8,374,220,19,13,10548,311,279,91592,1107,576,13173,11,419,3363,17767,282,4080,20,8,284,220,19,1124,3593,5338,11,582,27679,17767,856,284,481,20,1124,8,1119,279,47311,17767,282,2075,8,1124,7731,59,9640,69,4080,20,8,284,595,4080,20,29776,19,488,220,24,4080,20,29776,18,488,595,4080,20,29776,17,488,220,18,17,4080,20,8,481,220,16,16,198,59,2533],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe remainder when $kx^4+9x^3+kx^2+32x-11$ is divided by $x + 5$ is $4$.  Find $k.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cf11879ae816bb10bdc306e1676dd91dc84ffc17916b8d9bca5aa1a92731c94e"},"seconds":20.483909845352173,"seed":326481407098253687,"stop":"eos","text":"Calculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n(-5)^4 = 625 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k \\cdot 625 = 625k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-5)^3 = -125 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 9 \\cdot (-125) = -1125\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-5)^2 = 25 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k \\cdot 25 = 25k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n32 \\cdot (-5) = -160\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = 625k - 1125 + 25k - 160 - 11\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = 650k - 1296\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\( f(-5) = 4 \\), so we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nAdd 1296 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n650k = 1300\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 650:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1300}{650} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we will use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \\( f(x) \\) by \\( x - c \\) is \\( f(c) \\). In this case, we are dividing by \\( x + 5 \\), which can be written as \\( x - (-5) \\), so \\( c = -5 \\).\n\nGiven the polynomial \\( f(x) = kx^4 + 9x^3 + kx^2 + 32x - 11 \\), we need to find the value of \\( k \\) such that the remainder when \\( f(x) \\) is divided by \\( x + 5 \\) is 4. According to the Remainder Theorem, this means \\( f(-5) = 4 \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = -5 \\) into the polynomial \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = k(-5)^4 + 9(-5)^3 + k(-5)^2 + 32(-5) - 11\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n(-5)^4 = 625 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k \\cdot 625 = 625k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-5)^3 = -125 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 9 \\cdot (-125) = -1125\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-5)^2 = 25 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k \\cdot 25 = 25k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n32 \\cdot (-5) = -160\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = 625k - 1125 + 25k - 160 - 11\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = 650k - 1296\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\( f(-5) = 4 \\), so we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nAdd 1296 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n650k = 1300\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 650:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1300}{650} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:583"}
